{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["And the reason for <b>\"See, he brought us a Hebrew man\"</b> is that the Hebrews are detested by the Egyptians, and they cannot eat bread with them, for it is an abomination to them (Genesis 43:32), and they will buy from them only from vintners and vine-dressers, and they will not enter their houses. Therefore she said, \"See, he has done evil to us, to bring a Hebrew into our house and make him an officer and a ruler,\" and he deserves to mock us, as it is said (Proverbs 29:21), \"He who pampers his servant from youth…\" and so forth. And this is the reason for \"whom you brought to us,\" for his being brought into their house was grievous in their eyes. And she said \"he brought us\" referring to her husband; she did not mention him out of respect for his honor, or by way of instruction to the women, or because the one who brought him is well known. And similarly in the book of Job, in many places, it speaks of the honored Name in an unspecified manner, because it is understood that it speaks of Him. And similarly, \"And he said to Abner, Why have you come to my father's concubine?\" (II Samuel 3:7) — the speaker does not mention [the subject] and does not refer to him at all, because it is known that he is Ish-bosheth:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 39, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:42:08.947892121Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}